[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/semver_solver_multi/lib/dependency_solver_impl.py: register_package (line 46) crashes with ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: '¹' because the ad-hoc precedence key built by _identifier_key (lines 13-16) treats every string whose str.isdigit() is true as a numeric prerelease identifier and calls int() on it; SemVer 2.0.0 numeric prerelease identifiers are restricted to ASCII digits [0-9], so '¹' is a non-numeric identifier that must be ranked as an alphanumeric identifier through ASCII lexical comparison. The minimized fuzz case registers PackageRelease(name='', version=Version(major=0, minor=0, patch=0, prerelease=('¹',), build=())), which aborts instead of registering, violating the register_package postcondition in low/dependency_solver.pyi that it MUST register the release into the repository and the get_releases postcondition that releases MUST be ordered by version precedence descending; the author's CHANGE comment claims descending-precedence sorting but the implementation derives its own key instead of using the version engine comparison required by low/dependency_solver_impl.pyi, so numeric identifiers must be classified strictly by ASCII digits and all other identifiers compared lexically, never raising on directly constructed Version values.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/semver_solver_multi/lib/dependency_solver_impl.py: Resolve fuzz feedback in _identifier_key: classify prerelease identifiers as numeric only when they match strict ASCII digits [0-9] via a compiled regex, so non-ASCII digit-like identifiers such as '¹' fall through to ASCII lexical alphanumeric ranking instead of raising ValueError from int(). Numeric ordering now compares significant length then digit sequence rather than converting to int, so registers never abort on directly constructed Version values, preserving descending-precedence release ordering in register_package/get_releases.
